 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>The takomeshi (rice, octopus, ginger, dashi, light soy, mirin, salt, sake, green onion folded in after) and the sweet-soy baby octopus (salt rub, plain-water simmer first, then soy, mirin, sugar and water under a drop lid for 20 minutes) are both Okayama regional recipes as published by Japan's Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries; the cleaning follows Honda's fishing guide to iidako.</li><li>Takomeshi is commonly eaten as onigiri.</li><li><b>This site's additions:</b> baby octopus instead of octopus legs in the rice, keeping the simmered ones whole (the recipe cuts them into bite-size pieces), reducing the sauce to a glaze, the round shape with the octopus on top and the nori belt, the 10-minute plain-water simmer and the amounts for six.</li><li>Adjust to taste.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "What's established and what's this site's"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Iidako means “rice octopus”: in winter the heads are full of roe that looks like grains of cooked rice — a delicacy in Okayama.</li><li>If yours have it, you can leave it in: snip off the eyes and beak and pull out only the innards below the roe.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "“Rice octopus”"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Low heat for the first simmer, and never a hard boil.</li><li>The Okayama recipe also beats the tentacles lightly (with a piece of daikon) to soften them — worth doing if yours are on the large side.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Tender, not rubbery"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Use <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"100\" data-s=\"0\">100 g</span> of octopus legs, cut into <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"len\" data-u=\"cm\" data-a=\"1\" data-s=\"0\">1 cm</span> pieces, in the rice (that's the original takomeshi), and make plain round onigiri with a nori belt.</p>",
   "title_html": "No baby octopus?"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Best within a few hours, at cool room temperature.</li><li>For longer, keep the glazed octopus in the fridge and put it on just before eating — cold rice goes hard, so don't refrigerate the shaped onigiri.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Keeping them"
  }
 ],
